## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to reason with Kleene's three-valued logic and implement a lightweight backtracking propagator over a small, non-binary constraint set.

## Key Concepts
- **Kleene 3-valued semantics**: Values ordered T > U > F; negation of U remains U
- **Implication equivalence**: a→b = ¬a ∨ b
- **Constraint propagation**: Propagating partial information through implications
- **Goal**: Produce a non-F assignment that still utilizes the unknown value U

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v) {
  if (v === 'T') return 'F';
  if (v === 'F') return 'T';
  return 'U';
}

function or(a, b) {
  if (a === 'T' || b === 'T') return 'T';
  if (a === 'F' && b === 'F') return 'F';
  return 'U';
}

function and(a, b) {
  if (a === 'F' || b === 'F') return 'F';
  if (a === 'T' && b === 'T') return 'T';
  return 'U';
}

function evalRule(rule, vars) {
  const [a, b] = rule.map(v => vars[v]);
  return or(not(a), b);
}

function propagate(vars, rules) {
  for (const rule of rules) {
    const result = evalRule(rule, vars);
    if (result === 'F') return false;
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of least fixed-point computation in monotone boolean networks with thresholds and how to implement an efficient propagation algorithm that converges even with cycles.

## Key Concepts
- Monotone operators and least fixed points
- Threshold-based activation with in-neighbor counting
- Queue-driven BFS-like propagation
- Handling cycles via convergence to a fixpoint

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation of the propagation
function leastFixedPoint(T, edges, thresh) {
  const n = thresh.length;
  const succ = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u,v] of edges) succ[u].push(v);
  const count = Array(n).fill(0);
  const inSet = Array(n).fill(false);
  const q = [];
  for (const i of T) { inSet[i] = true; q.push(i); }
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const v of succ[u]) {
      count[v]++;
      if (!inSet[v] && count[v] >= thresh[v]) {
        inSet[v] = true;
        q.push(v);
      }
    }
  }
  return Array.from({length: n}, (_,i) => i).filter(i => inSet[i]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for weighted thresholds or non-monotone edges?
- How would you prove the result is the least fixed point containing T?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests three-valued reasoning under uncertainty, constraint propagation techniques, and handling rule priorities in practical policy evaluation systems.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic for handling unknown information
- Rule prioritization and conflict resolution
- Backtracking search with constraint propagation
- Truth functions: ¬U = U; ∧/∨ as min/max; A → B = ¬A ∨ B

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene three-valued logic operations
function kleeneNot(x) { 
  return x === 'T' ? 'F' : x === 'F' ? 'T' : 'U'; 
}

function kleeneAnd(a, b) { 
  return (a === 'F' || b === 'F') ? 'F' : 
         (a === 'T' && b === 'T') ? 'T' : 'U'; 
}

function kleeneOr(a, b) { 
  return (a === 'T' || b === 'T') ? 'T' : 
         (a === 'F' && b === 'F') ? 'F' : 'U'; 
}

function kleeneImplies(a, b) { 
  return kleeneOr(kleeneNot(a), b); 
}

// Rule evaluation with priority handling
function evaluateRule(rule, assignment) {
  const { antecedent, consequent } = rule;
  const antecedentValue = evaluateExpression(antecedent, assignment);
  const consequentValue = evaluateExpression(consequent, assignment);
  return kleeneImplies(antecedentValue, consequentValue);
}
```

## Solution Strategy
The backtracking algorithm systematically explores assignments while respecting Kleene semantics and rule priorities. At each step, it propagates constraints and prunes branches where higher-priority rules would evaluate to false. The search terminates when a satisfying assignment is found or all possibilities are exhausted.

## Why This Is Asked
Every candidate demonstrates logic reasoning and basic constraint solving relevant to planning validations in software.

## Key Concepts
- Propositional logic and implications (A→B means ¬A ∨ B)
- Satisfiability with small variable sets
- Backtracking with constraint propagation for pruning

## Code Example
```javascript
function isSatisfiable(constraints, numVars = 3) {
  const vars = Array.from({length: numVars}, (_, i) => String.fromCharCode(65 + i));

  function backtrack(index, assignment) {
    if (index === numVars) return constraints.every(fn => fn(assignment));

    for (const val of [0, 1]) {
      assignment[vars[index]] = val;
      if (constraints.every(fn => fn(assignment))) {
        if (backtrack(index + 1, assignment)) return true;
      }
    }
    return false;
  }

  return backtrack(0, {});
}

const constraints = [
  a => a.A === 0 || a.B === 1,
  a => a.B === 0 || a.C === 1,
  a => a.A === 1 || a.C === 1
];
console.log(isSatisfiable(constraints)); // true
```

